/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:302:\label{sec:kernel solver}
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:308:In a graph $G$, a \emph{fort} is a non-empty subset of vertices $F \subseteq V(G)$ such that no propagating vertex outside $F$ is adjacent to precisely one vertex in $F$.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:309:A power dominating set must be a hitting set of the family of all \emph{fort neighborhoods} in $G$, i.e., if $F$ is a fort and $S$ is a power dominating set, then $N[F] \cap S \neq \emptyset$.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:310:Conversely, if a hitting set for a family $\mathcal F$ of fort neighborhoods is not a power dominating set, then one can find an additional fort of $G$ whose neighborhood is not in $\mathcal F$.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:312:This yields the following algorithm.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:313:Start with some set $\mathcal F$ of fort neighborhoods.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:316:Otherwise, we construct at least one new fort neighborhood and add it to $\mathcal F$.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:318:One core ingredient of this approach is the choice of which fort neighborhoods to add to~$\mathcal F$.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:319:Previous approaches~\cite{smith2020OptimalSP,bozeman2018RestrictedPD} aimed at finding forts or fort neighborhoods that are as small as possible.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:320:The reasoning behind this is that the set of all fort neighborhoods can be exponentially large (even when restricted to those that are minimal with respect to inclusion) and thus it makes sense to add sets that are as restrictive as possible, hoping that only few sets suffice before the \HS solution yields a \Pds solution.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:321:However, finding forts of minimum size or minimum size fort neighborhoods is difficult while just finding any fort is easy.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:322:Moreover, if we add only few forts in every step, we have to potentially solve more \HS instances.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:323:We thus propose to instead find multiple forts at once and to add them all to the \HS instance.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:324:Our method of finding forts is based on the following lemma.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:327:  \label{lem:unobserved fort}
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:330:  Then the set of unobserved vertices $V \setminus R$ is a fort.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:333:  Assume $V \setminus R$ is not a fort.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:335:  This constradicts the exhaustive application of the propagation rule and thus the set of unobserved vertices is a fort.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:338:By Lemma~\ref{lem:unobserved fort}, whenever we have a candidate solution that does not yet observe all vertices, we obtain a new fort and can add its neighborhood to the \HS instance $\mathcal F$.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:339:For the new forts, we have two objectives.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:340:First, we want the new fort neighborhood to actually provide new restrictions, i.e., it should not be already hit by the minimum hitting set $H$ of $\mathcal F$.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:342:Secondly, we want the resulting forts (i.e., the number of unobserved vertices) to be small.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:347:Moreover, let $H$ be a minimum hitting set of the current set of fort neighborhoods.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:358:This gives us at least one and up to $\ell$ new fort neighborhoods.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:360:Instead, we first apply a simple local search to make sure that each fort is minimal with respect to inclusion.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:361:To this end, we iteratively re-select vertices from $U$ that had been removed before and check whether this still results in a non-empty fort.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:376:% This heuristic can be augmented by exhaustively applying our reduction rules in the beginning and each time we select another vertex.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/solving.tex:379:% \todoin{Move minimum size fort to appendix?}
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/experiments.tex:2:\label{sec:experiments}
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/experiments.tex:5:First, we evaluate the performance of our algorithm in comparison to two previous state-of-the-art approaches.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/experiments.tex:6:Secondly, we give a more detailed view on the performance by analyzing how the upper and lower bounds found be the different algorithms converge to the optimal solution.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/experiments.tex:7:Thirdly, we evaluate the impact of the different reduction rules.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/experiments.tex:11:We implemented our algorithm in C++ 20 and compiled it with clang 15.0.1 with the \texttt{-O3} optimization flag.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/experiments.tex:14:In the following, we refer to this algorithm with \texttt{MILP}.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/experiments.tex:16:Unfortunately, their code is not publicly available, and the paper does not specify all implementation details.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/experiments.tex:17:To make a fair (or rather generous) comparison, we initialized their set of forts with our fort heuristic, which, as far as we can judge, leads to better results than reported in the original publication~\cite{smith2020OptimalSP}.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/experiments.tex:18:We refer to this algorithm as \texttt{MFN} (abbreviation for \emph{minimum fort neighborhood}).
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/experiments.tex:35:We compare the performance of our solver to the \texttt{MILP} and \texttt{MFN} approach, each with and without preprocessing by the reduction rules.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/experiments.tex:36:To assess the performance of our approach with reduction rules, we compute the speedup compared to the lowest run time of the previous approaches without reduction rules.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/experiments.tex:39:\caption{Run times of different combinations of \Pds solvers and reduction rules on the pandapower data set. Note that $\gamma_P$ differs from the results reported in other literature. This is to be expected because we include non-propagating vertices from the input. Further observe that some run times are given in milli- or microseconds.}
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/experiments.tex:40:\label{tbl:solver comparison normal}
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/experiments.tex:73:  \item[c] solved optimally by reduction rules
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/experiments.tex:79:Preprocessing significantly reduced the running times of all solvers in most cases, especially for the larger instances.
/tmp/pds-paper-src/sections/experiments.tex:80:In fact, we found that our reduction rules were able to solve 9 out of 26 instances on their own.
